            'azure_devops_security_access_control_entries_set_access_control_entries' => [
                'class' => AzureDevOpsSecurityAccessControlEntriesSetAccessControlEntries::class,
                'name' => 'Add or update ACEs in the ACL for the provided token. The request body contains the target token, a list of [ACEs](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/azure/devops/security/access-control-entries/set-access-control-entries?#accesscontrolentry) and a optional merge parameter. In the case of a collision (by identity descriptor) with an existing ACE in the ACL, the "merge" parameter determines the behavior. If set, the existing ACE has its allow and deny merged with the incoming ACE\'s allow and deny. If unset, the existing ACE is displaced. For optimal performance and reliability, it is strongly recommended to batch multiple ACEs in a single request rather than sending individual requests. Batching requests improves efficiency, reduces overhead, and helps ensure successful completion of your operations.',
                'description' => 'Add or update ACEs in the ACL for the provided token. The request body contains the target token, a list of [ACEs](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/azure/devops/security/access-control-entries/set-access-control-entries?#accesscontrolentry) and a optional merge parameter. In the case of a collision (by identity descriptor) with an existing ACE in the ACL, the "merge" parameter determines the behavior. If set, the existing ACE has its allow and deny merged with the incoming ACE\'s allow and deny. If unset, the existing ACE is displaced. For optimal performance and reliability, it is strongly recommended to batch multiple ACEs in a single request rather than sending individual requests. Batching requests improves efficiency, reduces overhead, and helps ensure successful completion of your operations.

